d together many  were naked except for a blanket and  there was no heating or hot water  all in all it's fair to say there wasn't  much chance of anybody at bethlehem  actually getting cured of anything  the following few decades saw the board  of governors at bethlehem opposed the  reforms imposed upon them by the  parliamentary committee  but their resistance ended in 1851 when  the institution was finally taken into  public hands  ending six centuries of independence  the hospital's notoriety was gradually  brought under control and in 1930 the  establishment was moved to its  present-day location in kent  as conditions improved so the nickname  bedlam faded  most people who use the word these days  have no idea where it came from  but perhaps that's for the best  in a world where treatment for the  mentally ill is becoming better  understood with every passing year  some horrors are better left in the past  where they belong  thanks for watching

